<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:name=".SplashActivity"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.Splash"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <!--
              Links in our emails open the app if it is installed.

              Every link Taxify sends — activation, password reset, an
              accountant invitation, a support ticket — points at
              taxify.mikesapphub.com, and the app *is* that site loaded in a
              webview. So a link tapped on a phone with the app installed was
              still opening a browser, signing in a second time, and leaving
              the app it was already signed into sitting in the background.

              autoVerify makes Android check /.well-known/assetlinks.json on
              that host at install time. It only hands us the link if the file
              lists this package and the certificate this build was signed
              with — which is why nothing here can hijack somebody else's
              domain, and why the file has to carry the real fingerprint or
              verification silently fails and links keep going to the browser.

              No app installed, or verification failed: the browser opens it,
              exactly as before. There is no case where the link stops working.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <!-- Only the app, not the whole site.
                     Without a path prefix the app also claims the landing
                     page, so somebody tapping a link to the marketing site
                     from a text message would be dropped into a signed-in
                     dashboard instead of the page they were sent. -->
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="taxify.mikesapphub.com" android:pathPrefix="/app" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          What a pushed notification looks like when the app is not running.
          Without these Android draws the launcher icon squashed into the status
          bar, which comes out as a grey square.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_icon"
            android:resource="@drawable/ic_stat_taxify" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_color"
            android:resource="@color/brand_blue" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_channel_id"
            android:value="taxify_default" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
      Why "Take photo" opened a file picker instead of the camera.

      Tapping it puts a capture request to the webview, and Capacitor answers it
      by building an ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE intent and asking
      resolveActivity() which app would handle it. From Android 11 onwards an
      app cannot see another app it has not declared an interest in, so that
      call came back null, Capacitor decided no camera existed, and quietly fell
      back to the ordinary file picker. Nothing failed and nothing was logged
      where anybody would see it — it just was not the camera.

      Declaring the intent here is what makes the camera visible to us. It is a
      visibility declaration and not a permission: it grants nothing, and only
      lets us ask who handles taking a photo.

      This intent is the fallback now rather than the usual path, and the
      reason is which lens it opens. Capacitor reads capture="environment" as a
      yes or no — "the page wants the camera" — and builds this intent carrying
      nothing but an output path. Which camera comes up is then entirely the
      camera app's decision, and on plenty of phones that is the front one,
      which is no use for photographing a receipt. So the app takes the photo
      itself now, through getUserMedia, where facingMode really does select the
      rear lens. The reasoning in full is at the top of CameraCapture.jsx.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.IMAGE_CAPTURE" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
      CAMERA, having deliberately not been declared until now.

      getUserMedia in a webview asks the host for VIDEO_CAPTURE, and Capacitor
      answers that by requesting CAMERA at runtime — which Android only allows
      for a permission declared here. Without this line the in-app camera can
      never be granted and every photo falls back to the intent above, with the
      wrong lens.

      One consequence, written down so it does not have to be rediscovered:
      Capacitor treats a *declared* CAMERA permission as one it must also hold
      before it will use that fallback intent, so the fallback now asks first
      where it used to go straight through. It is the same prompt getUserMedia
      raises, on a path that is only reached when the in-app camera cannot run.

      Not required: the device does not have to have a camera. Somebody on a
      tablet without one can still attach a file.
    -->
    <!--
      Only for Android 9 and below, where writing a download into the public
      Downloads folder still needed permission. From Android 10 the download
      manager handles that itself, which is why this is capped — asking a
      modern phone for storage access it does not need is how an app gets
      declined. MainActivity falls back to its own folder if the write is
      refused anyway.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="28" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
</manifest>
